{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And if thus You do to me.</b> Rashi explained that Moses' strength failed like a woman's. And many, when they saw this, were astonished at this interpretation: is it because Moses' strength failed like a woman's that he used the feminine form \"אַתְּ\" toward above? And I say that this is not so very difficult, for our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yalkut Shimoni, Ha'azinu, remez 945) that the righteous add strength to the power above, as it says (Numbers 14:17): ״וְעַתָּה יִגְדַּל נָא כֹּחַ ה׳״, and the wicked weaken His strength, as it says (Deuteronomy 32:18): ״צוּר יְלָדְךָ תֶּשִׁי״. And the explanation of the matter is that from the side of the interruption of influence caused by the deeds of the wicked, it appears as though there is a weakening of strength, as it were. And here, because the hands of the Beloved slackened and His strength failed like a woman's, there was no strength in Him to add strength to the power above. Therefore He used the feminine form \"אַתְּ\" toward above, to indicate that the one depends upon the other — הָא בְּהָא תַּלְיָא. And this is a precious interpretation." "And it is plausible to hear that it refers to what he said, ״כַּאֲשֶׁר יִשָּׂא הָאֹמֵן״ — and he did not say אוֹמֶנֶת. Mahar<i>Y</i>A explained: You do to me like a male nurse (אוֹמֵן) who carries the suckling, who has no breasts to nurse the child when he cries — so too it is not in my hand to fulfill their demand. And concerning this it was said, ״וְאִם כָּכָה אַתְּ עֹשֶׂה לִי״: for this likeness of a female is found in You, as it were, since it is in Your hand to satisfy their thirst, yet You act the man, so how do You cast upon me the likeness of the female, when I am a male who has no breasts to nurse? And the likeness of \"אַתְּ\" toward above is not a weakening of strength, heaven forbid, but rather indicates the capacity of a female, who has breasts to nurse."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "Mahar\"i A = R. Joseph Taitazak (or possibly R. Joseph Ashkenazi); the abbreviation מַהֲרִי״א resolved as Mahar<i>Y</i>A per the text's internal logic about the masculine form אֹמֵן."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:46.345989362Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}